# Architect review — byte-sized index page cuts

Scope read, read-only, no tests run: both artifacts in full; [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:286), [btree.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:1998), [storage.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree/storage.cljc:93), [cbor.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:913), [jing.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:153), [yin/repl/index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:356), [dao.jing.dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:73), [dao.jing.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:798), branch commits `4f772d61` and `cb2cbe11`, and the uncommitted sweep state in `/tmp/nrf-branch-wt`.

## Overall disposition: APPROVED-WITH-CHANGES

The reframe is approved. The brief as written is not implementable: its sizing algorithm, its branch accounting, its manifest proposal, and its revert step each fail against the code. Required changes R1–R7 below are precise enough to re-brief from. Do not implement the brief verbatim.

## 1. Framing — APPROVED, with one correction

Byte-sized cutting at the index builder is the correct axis. The bound that bites is a byte bound on the encoded `:store` message ([dao.jing.dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:228): `store-message-size`, refused in `send-datagram!` at [dao.jing.dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:254)). No count cap can respect it, because datom values are unbounded in size. Fix 5 (`4f772d61`, `:branching-factor 64`) only moves the failure to larger code values.

The alternatives are worse, and the review considered them:

- Raising `max-message-bytes` alone: the design doc already marks it composition data to “confirm or raise” ([dao.jing.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:805)), and S3 confirmed 65,536 covers only a small-datom build (26,497-byte canonical value, 26,720-byte wire value). Raising the bound does not bound a count-cut page; the same bug recurs at larger values, with larger reassembly state (`max-partial-messages` 64).
- DHT chunking: already exists for messages over `max-datagram` (1200) and is explicitly capped by `max-message-bytes` before splitting. It cannot carry an over-bound store message.
- Oversize-value chunking at another layer: a datom is the atomic index key in all four orders (`eavt-cmp`/`aevt-cmp`/`avet-cmp`/`vaet-cmp`). Splitting a value across leaves breaks ordering, lookup, and set semantics. Wrong layer.

Correction to the directive: “fixed max-message-bound-derived cut” is not an alternative to byte cutting; it is byte cutting. The owner’s framing stands.

## 2. Cut algorithm — REJECTED as specified; replacement specified

Determinism: preserved either way. Canonical CBOR is a pure function of the value ([cbor.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:913) `encode`; [jing.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:153) `canonical-bytes`), and `from-sequential` sorts and dedupes before building ([btree.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:2059)). A size derived from those bytes is a pure function of the sorted stream.

Cost: encode-candidate-per-datom is O(k²·s) per leaf — each of k datoms re-encodes the whole accumulated leaf. At REPL page sizes this is the dominant build cost and heavy allocation churn on JS/Dart. Unacceptable when an exact O(n) accounting exists.

Required replacement (R1): encode once, sum lengths.

- Encode each sorted datom once; keep its byte length.
- Leaf blob is `{:keys [d…]}` ([btree.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:1080) `node->blob`). Its encoded size is exactly: map framing + `:keys` identifier bytes + array header for n + Σ datom lengths. Recompute only the array header as n crosses CBOR header thresholds (24, 256, 65536). Cut when adding the next datom would exceed the budget. No candidate re-encodes.
- This is exact, not an estimate, because CBOR concatenation is additive apart from the header. State that proof in the design; forbid `pr-str` lengths or host string lengths anywhere in the accounting. Byte lengths come from the host byte array only.
- Keep the brief’s oversize rule: a single datom over budget still forms a one-datom leaf, and the store refusal remains the last resort. Add a test that pins this as a loud refusal, not a silent unacknowledged publication (see Missed, below).

## 3. Bulk vs incremental — FAILS as briefed; design addition required

The brief heads its work “`from-sequential` and the publish path,” then specifies only the bulk builder. The two paths are different code:

- Bulk: [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:564) `publish-index!` → `bt/from-sequential` with `{:branching-factor branching}`.
- Incremental: `session`/`fold-batch` folds with `bt/conj` ([index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:1034)), and [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:1142) `publish!` serializes the existing tree via `bt/store-tree`. Splits there obey only `Settings.branching-factor` (`bf*`/`min-bf*`, [btree.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:75)). The REPL uses both: full reconstruction via `transactor/publish!`, suffix publication via `index/publish!` ([yin/repl/index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:394)).

So a bulk-only byte cut leaves incremental publications able to emit the same 70 KiB leaf and collapse the same way. That alone would reject the brief.

One premise in the review prompt needs correcting: differently-shaped trees from the two paths do not break an invariant. The design already disavows cross-path manifest equality — bulk and `conj` partition the same rows differently, and acceptance is logical equality ([dao.space.index.as-observer.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md:685), item A9). Determinism is per-path: same sorted stream plus same budget gives same tree, on each path separately.

Required addition (R2): enforce the bound at blob emission on both paths, without redesigning the B-tree in this slice.

- Keep `Settings` count-based. Do not make `node-conj`/`node-disj` byte-aware here; that rewrites split/merge/borrow invariants and is a separate design.
- Bulk path: byte-aware grouping in `from-sorted-array`/`from-sequential` per R1.
- Incremental path: `publish!` already holds every emitted blob’s canonical bytes in the recording handle (`:order` of `[address bs]`, [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:493)). Check each `bs` length against the budget there — zero extra encodes. On violation, rebuild that index from `bt/seq` through the byte-cut bulk builder and store that instead. Never emit an over-budget blob and never count such a publication as published.
- Thread the budget through `session`, `restore`, and the REPL `:publish-opts` so both paths see the same number.

## 4. Manifest compatibility — brief’s preferred option REJECTED

“Keep `:branching-factor` as the realized emergent count, no schema change” changes the field’s meaning while every consumer still reads the old one:

- Validator requires an integer ≥ 2 ([index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:286)). An emergent root fanout is 0 for an empty tree and below 2 for a single-leaf tree. Those manifests become invalid.
- `restored-indexes` threads the field into `kv-storage` Settings so restored nodes “split at the published thresholds” ([index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:341)); `verify-candidate` and `restore` do the same ([index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:1310), [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:1344)). An emergent 3 or 7 would make all later mutation split at 3 or 7.
- The verified-frontier gate compares the manifest field to `:publish-opts` for equality before reusing a suffix frontier ([yin/repl/index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:368)). An emergent value varies per publication, so the gate would fail almost every round and force full reconstruction — or, if “fixed” by deleting the comparison, would reuse frontiers without the check that validates them. Do not remove that comparison.
- One field cannot describe four trees. Byte cuts give EAVT/AEVT/AVET/VAET different leaf counts over the same datoms. There is no single emergent count to record.
- The manifest is a hash input (`jing/segment-key manifest`, [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:619)). Redefining the field churns every manifest address and defeats the cache-sharing the directive wants to preserve, for scalar trees that never had the problem.

Required (R3): keep the dual constraint and the schema. Cut a page when either the byte budget or the count cap (512) is reached. `:branching-factor` keeps meaning “the count cap this tree was built and must be restored with”; the manifest stays exactly `{:indexes :count :branching-factor}`. If the budget must later be recorded for restore fidelity, that is a schema proposal to the work log per the directive’s own rule — not this slice, and not a reason to overload the existing field.

## 5. Reverting `cb2cbe11` (`:repair-slots 48`) — REJECTED as a step of this change

The brief’s hedge (“verify rather than assume”) is right; its presumption is wrong, and its premise — “the 8× amplification collapses” — does not hold. At a 48 KiB budget, code-heavy REPL pages hold roughly the same datom counts as fix 5’s 64-key pages, so per-round address counts stay in fix 5’s 16–34 range, not the 512-era 9–16. The directive itself already says this correctly: page count per round still scales with volume, so the cadence question remains open.

The live evidence also already superseded the brief: `/tmp/nrf-branch-wt` carries an uncommitted sweep replacing 48 with a measured 40 (49 idle ticks in a 400-tick observation, against 32 at 48 and 3 at 32; queue peaks 221 at 16 slots, 133 at 24, 81 at 32). A blanket `git revert cb2cbe11` returns the store to the `dao.space.dht` default of 16 — the configuration measured never to drain.

Required (R4): no revert in the byte-cut change. After byte-cut lands, re-run the dart ticker-idle test at 16, 40, and 48 with byte-cut pages and pin the measured winner. Retire fix 5’s `:branching-factor 64` override in the same change (R3 makes it redundant); keep its commit in history, as the brief says.

## 6. Sequencing — implement now on `nightly-reds-fix` (owner redirect upheld)

The directive’s “after master-green, do not fold into nightly-reds-fix” was written before the owner halted the slot sweep as solving the wrong problem. Merging the branch as-is would land a known-wrong workaround (64-key pages plus slot tuning fitted to them) and then immediately rework it. The branch is also the only place the failing configuration reproduces. Implement byte-cut there, with independent review before merge.

Risks owned with this pick: the change touches bulk-build behavior on a nightly-fix branch, so the merge waits on the brief’s full dart + node matrix plus the index namespaces; pin churn (see Missed) must be reported as measured emergent counts, never fitted. Worktree hygiene needs care first: `/tmp/nrf-branch-wt` is detached at `4f772d61` with the uncommitted 40-slot edit, while `nightly-reds-fix` names `cb2cbe11`. Reconcile that state explicitly before any implementation commit; do not let the slot edit ride along silently.

The alternative’s risk is worse: master-green merges fix 5, the repair cadence gets tuned to the wrong page shape a second time, and the byte work starts over on a new slice.

## 7. The 48 KiB budget — DEMAND MEASUREMENT; number not confirmed

48 KiB is safe but unjustified, and as specified it is wired wrong:

- It hardcodes 49,152 in `dao.space.index`, while the bound it derives from is per-composition data (`::max-message-bytes` in [dao.jing.dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:73), overridable at composition). Raise the composition bound and the index still over-cuts; lower it and the index overflows. Required (R5): derive the budget at the publish boundary — budget = composition `max-message-bytes` − measured `:store` envelope − margin — and pass it through `:publish-opts`. The envelope oracle already exists: `store-message-size` with maximal `q` and full cookie ([dao.jing.dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:228)). Tests must assert against the full store message, not the leaf alone.
- The headroom is far larger than the envelope needs. The only measurement on record shows 223 bytes of envelope (26,497 → 26,720). Reserving 16 KiB inflates page count by roughly a third versus a ~60 KiB budget, which directly worsens the repair economics in §5. But S3 measured small datoms only; no REPL code-datom leaf distribution has been measured at any budget.
- Required (R6): before pinning a number, measure real REPL covered-index leaves (the dart `yin.repl.dht-test` tree) — leaf byte distribution, emergent counts per index, per-round address counts. Then set the budget from the measured envelope plus an stated margin. 49,152 may stand as a provisional conservative pin only if the brief says that is what it is.

## What both artifacts missed entirely

1. Branch sizing is undercounted in the brief. A branch blob is `{:level n :keys [separator datoms] :addresses [...]}` ([btree.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:1080)). “Each entry’s bytes = its canonical address encoding” omits the separator keys — full datoms, the largest part — plus map/array framing. Branch cuts must account the whole blob with the R1 summation method, or branches overflow after leaves are fixed.
2. Bulk fill invariants. `split-ranges` builds chunks between `min-bf` and `max` aiming at the average ([btree.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:1998)), and branch levels group child nodes by the same count ranges. Byte grouping replaces this wholesale. The design must state that byte-cut leaves may be underfull by count, that this is legal for published immutable trees, and how a restored underfull leaf then behaves under `conj`/`disj` borrow/merge at `min-bf = bf/2`.
3. Oversize publication semantics. `publish-index!` appends blobs to the intake before the DHT ever refuses them; an over-budget leaf therefore yields a locally durable, remotely unacknowledged manifest — the original symptom in quieter form. The design must surface that as a publication failure at publish time (R2’s emission check does), not leave it to ack collapse.
4. Budget threading for the incremental session. The REPL indexer’s `:publish-opts` reach only the full-reconstruction path; the suffix path’s session is rebuilt by `index/restore` from the manifest. Unless the budget is threaded through `restore`/`session` and the storage Settings’ composition, the two paths will silently use different budgets after a restart.
5. Test pins beyond the four shape pins. Grep shows the load-bearing ones: the default-factor pin `(= 512 (:branching-factor manifest))` and the many `{:branching-factor 4}` fixtures in [index_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/space/index_test.cljc:163), the manifest-shape assertions at [index_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/space/index_test.cljc:573), and the DHT test’s `(= 64 … :branching-factor)` pin added by fix 5. Under R3 the 512/4 semantics survive; only the fix-5 pin and any realized-count assertions change, each reported with its measured value per measure-then-pin.
6. The manifest blob itself and non-DHT intake. Pages also travel intake streams and local stores that have no 64 KiB bound. Byte cutting is harmless there, but the design should say the budget is a DHT-derived composition input (R5), not a new property of the index format — otherwise a future non-DHT composition inherits an unexplained page shape.

## Required changes for the re-brief

- R1: encode-once length summation for leaf and branch cuts; exact header accounting; no candidate re-encodes (§2).
- R2: emission-bound check plus bulk-rebuild fallback on the incremental `publish!` path; budget threaded through `session`/`restore` (§3, Missed 4).
- R3: dual constraint (byte budget and 512 count cap); `:branching-factor` semantics and manifest schema unchanged; retire only the fix-5 `64` override (§4, §5).
- R4: no `cb2cbe11` revert in this change; slot count re-measured after landing (§5).
- R5: budget derived from the composition’s `max-message-bytes` minus the `store-message-size` envelope, passed via `:publish-opts`; tests assert full store-message size (§7).
- R6: REPL leaf measurement precedes pinning the budget number; 49,152 provisional only (§7).
- R7: sequence on `nightly-reds-fix` now, after reconciling the detached worktree and its uncommitted 40-slot edit; full dart + node matrix before merge (§6).

With R1–R7 folded in, this is approved to implement.
